course: dictionary
lesson: 12514
title: Derive: where something comes from
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To derive something from something means to get it from there. «Sam derives pleasure from reading at night.» The word from is the clue: Sam gets his pleasure from reading.
explain: Derive can also mean to come from something. «The word telescope derives from Greek.» This means the word comes from Greek. When you see derive or derived with from, look for where something started.
explain: In maths and science, to derive a rule means to work it out step by step from what you already know. «Ava derived the rule by working it out one step at a time.» She did not guess. She built the rule carefully from facts she already knew.
fact: Derive is a verb: derive, derives, derived, deriving.
fact: «Derive from» and «derived from» mean come from: «The word derives from Latin.»
fact: «Derive pleasure from» means to get pleasure from something: «Sam derives pleasure from reading.»
fact: Many English words derive from Latin or Greek.
fact: In maths, to derive a formula means to work it out step by step from what you already know.
exercise: «Sam derives pleasure from reading at night.» What does derives pleasure from mean? | *Gets pleasure from | Hates reading at night | Reads very slowly | Loses his book
exercise: «The word telescope derives from Greek.» What does derives from mean? | Is written in a book | Goes to the shop | *Comes from | Is longer than
exercise: «Ava derived the rule by working it out one step at a time.» How did Ava get the rule? | She forgot it straight away | She copied it from a friend | *She worked it out step by step | She guessed it at random
test: Nadia says derive means to draw a picture. What does derive really mean? | *Get from something, or work out step by step | Give a present | Go to sleep | Draw a picture
test: «The word kindergarten derives from German.» Where does kindergarten come from? | *German | Spanish | Greek | French
exam: Which sentence uses derive to mean work out step by step? | Tom forgot the formula | Tom ate his dinner one step at a time | *Tom derived the formula one step at a time | Tom drew a map of the park
